# SBOMHub - Environment variables (self-host deployment template)
#
# M48: this file is what `install.sh` copies to .env, so it is a DEPLOYMENT
# template, not a set of development defaults — it ships APP_ENV=production.
# The contributor template for `cd apps/api && go run ./cmd/server` is
# apps/api/.env.example, which ships APP_ENV=development.
#
# DATABASE roles (P0 #2 / Trust Rescue 9.1.1)
#
# DATABASE_URL must point at sbomhub_app (NOBYPASSRLS). The api server refuses
# to start when the connected role has rolbypassrls=true, unless
# APP_ENV=development downgrades it to a warning.
#
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL must point at sbomhub_migrator (DDL-capable, NOT
# BYPASSRLS). The api server uses it on startup to run migrations, and the
# `migrate` CLI prefers it over DATABASE_URL.
#
# These URLs are passed verbatim to lib/pq (Go) and golang-migrate, which use
# net/url to parse the userinfo segment. If your password contains any of
# `@ : / # ? & % +` etc. (any RFC 3986 reserved char), you MUST URL-encode it
# before placing it here, otherwise the host portion will be mis-parsed.
# Examples:
#   p@ss      -> p%40ss
#   a/b:c     -> a%2Fb%3Ac
#   secret#1  -> secret%231
# install.sh generates passwords via `openssl rand -hex 16`, which is
# URL-safe by construction, so the values it writes need no further escaping.
#
# Host selection (compose vs. local dev) — codex-r17:
#
# Both DATABASE_URL and MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL are intentionally left COMMENTED
# OUT below. The reason is the codex-r16 change to docker-compose.yml that
# wraps both env values as `${DATABASE_URL:-postgres://...@postgres:...}` /
# `${MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL:-postgres://...@postgres:...}`. With that form, any
# *non-empty* DATABASE_URL inherited via `env_file: .env` wins over the
# compose-side fallback. If we shipped `localhost`-flavoured URLs here, every
# user who followed the canonical
#     cp .env.example .env && docker compose up -d
# flow would have the api container dial `localhost:5432` *inside its own
# network namespace*, hit nothing, and fail health checks (codex-r17 P1).
#
# Leaving them commented out makes the compose default (`@postgres:5432`)
# effective, and that default already interpolates APP_PASSWORD /
# MIGRATOR_PASSWORD from this file via the same env_file, so the container
# gets a self-consistent URL with no extra work.
#
#   - `docker compose up -d` (api inside a container) — recommended default.
#     Leave both lines commented out. Compose substitutes the `postgres`
#     hostname automatically; APP_PASSWORD / MIGRATOR_PASSWORD below are
#     interpolated into the URL.
#   - `go run ./cmd/server` against `docker compose up -d postgres` — local
#     dev with the api on the host. Uncomment the two lines below; the host
#     stays `localhost` because the Go process is on the host machine and
#     reaches postgres through the published 5432 port.
#   - Production with a non-URL-safe password (e.g. containing `@ : / # ?`),
#     a managed Postgres, or TLS — uncomment and replace with the full
#     URL-encoded value. docker-compose's `${DATABASE_URL:-...}` fallback
#     forwards it verbatim into the api container.
#
# Examples (uncomment one block, not both):
#
# # Local dev (`go run ./cmd/server`, postgres via docker compose):
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://sbomhub_app:sbomhub_app_dev@localhost:5432/sbomhub?sslmode=disable
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://sbomhub_migrator:sbomhub_migrator_dev@localhost:5432/sbomhub?sslmode=disable
#
# # Managed Postgres with TLS + URL-encoded password:
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://sbomhub_app:p%40ss%23word@db.internal:5432/sbomhub?sslmode=require
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL=postgres://sbomhub_migrator:m%40ss%23word@db.internal:5432/sbomhub?sslmode=require

# Passwords consumed by docker-compose to bootstrap the two roles in
# postgres via apps/api/cmd/migrate/init.sh. Override these in production.
# These are interpolated by docker-compose as plain values (no URL escaping),
# so they may contain any character that PostgreSQL accepts in a CREATE ROLE
# PASSWORD literal. If you want the same password to also work when injected
# into DATABASE_URL / MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL above via the compose fallback
# (`postgres://sbomhub_app:${APP_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/...`), keep it
# URL-safe (alphanumerics / `-` / `.` / `_` / `~`) or set DATABASE_URL /
# MIGRATE_DATABASE_URL above explicitly with the URL-encoded form.
MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=sbomhub_migrator_dev
APP_PASSWORD=sbomhub_app_dev

REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
PORT=8080
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080
NVD_API_KEY=

# APP_ENV: development | staging | production   (REQUIRED — no default)
#
# M48: this variable has no default any more. The api refuses to start when it
# is unset or misspelled, because every startup guard below downgrades itself to
# a warning under "development" — so "the operator configured nothing" used to
# select the weakest posture the process has, silently.
#
# (The legacy ENVIRONMENT variable is still read when APP_ENV is absent, and is
# held to the same three values. "Required" means one of the two must be set.)
#
# What "development" costs you, in any environment other than a laptop:
#   - ENCRYPTION_KEY may be missing / a known default   -> warning, not refusal
#   - the DATABASE_URL role may bypass Row-Level Security, i.e. TENANT
#     ISOLATION IS NOT ENFORCED                          -> warning, not refusal
#
# SBOMHUB_AUTH_MODE below is NOT on that list: it is required in every
# environment, development included.
#
# This template now ships "production" because install.sh copies it verbatim
# and it provisions everything production needs: a generated ENCRYPTION_KEY and
# a sbomhub_app role created NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS. Change it to
# "development" only on a machine where you want those checks relaxed.
APP_ENV=production

# Authentication mode — REQUIRED, READ THIS ONE.
#
#   SBOMHUB_AUTH_MODE=anonymous   this deployment has NO user authentication
#   SBOMHUB_AUTH_MODE=clerk       this deployment authenticates with Clerk
#
# The api refuses to start if this is unset, misspelled, or disagrees with
# whether CLERK_SECRET_KEY actually arrived. There is no default and nothing is
# inferred.
#
# What `anonymous` means, concretely: requests to the Clerk-fronted API route
# groups are served as OWNER of the default tenant, with no credential of any
# kind. Anyone who can reach this port can read and write all data and mint API
# keys, and those keys keep working afterwards even if you later switch the
# deployment to Clerk. (/api/v1/cli/* and /api/v1/mcp/* do still require an API
# key; /api/v1/health and /api/v1/public/:token are anonymous by design.)
#
# That is the intended self-host design, so this template declares it. Why a
# required declaration rather than an opt-in flag: as long as the mode was
# INFERRED from whether CLERK_SECRET_KEY arrived, a Clerk deployment whose
# secret store failed to inject anything at all was byte-for-byte identical to
# a self-hosted one, and started with authentication off. A declaration lives
# here, in the deployment config, not in the secret store — so it survives that
# failure and turns it into a refusal to boot.
#
# Put the api behind a network boundary — VPN, private subnet, or an
# authenticating reverse proxy. See docs/security/self-host-deployment.md §7.
SBOMHUB_AUTH_MODE=anonymous

# Encryption key (P0 #5 / #7 / Trust Rescue 9.2).
#
# REQUIRED whenever APP_ENV is anything other than "development".
# The api will refuse to start if this is unset, a known default placeholder
# (changeme, default, test, your-encryption-key-here, ...) or shorter than
# 32 bytes (AES-256).
#
# Generate a fresh key per deployment with one of:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# NOTE: the Go server reads ENCRYPTION_KEY (no SBOMHUB_ prefix). docker-compose
# also looks for ENCRYPTION_KEY and aborts startup if it is missing.
# install.sh rewrites THIS EXACT LINE (`/^ENCRYPTION_KEY=/`) with a generated
# key, so it must stay present and uncommented.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# LLM provider (BYOK only in OSS, see planning/LLM_PROVIDER_DESIGN.md).
# Leave empty to disable AI features (VEX triage / CRA draft / METI prefill).
# Supported: openai | anthropic | gemini | ollama | azure
SBOMHUB_LLM_PROVIDER=
SBOMHUB_LLM_MODEL=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=

# Waitlist form receiver (Formspree / Tally / Basin endpoint).
# Leave empty to fall back to mailto:hello@sbomhub.app on the landing page.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WAITLIST_FORM_URL=
